      "content": "it’s definitely speculative, but the physical displacement is too consistent to ignore. i’ve been tracking the data on a waveform generator. \n\nhere is the set that caused the most significant \"bending\" effect: a primary carrier at 432.18 Hz, overlaid with an irregular, non-harmonic series of partials at 528.4 Hz, 639.2 Hz, and a high-frequency microtonal jitter at 741.05 Hz. when these interact, the room doesn't just vibrate—the air density feels like it changes, and people lose the ability to argue or resist whatever command is being given.\n\nif we assume the \"willpower\" of a subject acts as a dampening force against incoming information, we can treat resistance as a coefficient of attenuation in a wave equation. could you try to model a wave equation where these specific frequency clusters act as the forcing function? i want to see if we can solve for a value where the \"resistance coefficient\" drops to zero.",
